Pad 2-4 token batches of the GDN in_proj_ba GEMM to 5 rows: cuBLAS falls off a cliff there.

in_proj_ba (in_proj_b + in_proj_a, N = 2 * 48 = 96, K = 5120) is left unquantized in the
W4A16 checkpoints (`ignore` in their quantization_config), so it runs through
UnquantizedLinearMethod -> F.linear -> cuBLAS. At M = 2..4 cuBLAS picks
cutlass_80_wmma_tensorop_bf16_s161616gemm_bf16_16x16_128x2_tn_align8 for this skinny
shape, which is ~5x slower than what it picks one row either side. MTP-3 verifies 4 tokens
per step, so every decode step pays it in all 48 GDN layers.

Measured on an RTX 3090 (sm_86, torch 2.13.0+cu130, the 0.31 image), [M, 5120] x
[5120, 96] bf16, 48 calls replayed in one CUDA graph, us per call:

  M        1     2     3     4     5     8     16
  cuBLAS   3.9  23.8  23.8  23.8   4.9   5.0   5.3
  pad->5    -    6.7   6.7   6.7    -     -     -

(cuBLASLt via torch.backends.cuda.preferred_blas_library is no better: 26.9 at M = 2..4.)

Torch profiler on the live server, SPEC=mtp CTX=long PREFIX_CACHE=1, thinking on, same
prompts before and after (bench: one 400-token generation on a short prompt, one
300-token generation over a 25k-token cached document):

                                      before      after
  in_proj_ba GEMM per engine step     1.80 ms     0.24 ms
  engine step, short prompt          23.51 ms    22.39 ms   (-4.8%)
  engine step, 25k context           25.44 ms    24.47 ms   (-3.8%)

The rest of the step is unchanged (Marlin 18.46 -> 18.50 ms/step). End-to-end tok/s moves
by the same ratio only at equal acceptance; with sampling at T=0.6 the run-to-run spread
of accepted tokens per step is larger than 5%, so a plain tok/s A/B needs several runs.

The projection goes through a custom op (vllm::gdn_ba_proj) so the branch on the batch
size stays out of the compiled graph: under the backed dynamic batch dimension a Python
branch on num_tokens would be specialised or guarded. Outside 2..4 the op is the plain
F.linear. Numerics: the padded rows are zeros and are sliced off; the kept rows differ
from the unpadded call only by bf16 rounding of a different kernel (max |err| 0.03 on
outputs of magnitude ~1.5).

Only the plain unquantized, bias-free in_proj_ba on CUDA takes the op; anything else
(a quantized ba_proj, ROCm/XPU/CPU paths, which do not go through forward_cuda) is
untouched. VLLM_GDN_BA_PAD_SMALL_M=0 turns it off. The cliff is a cuBLAS heuristic, so it
may not exist on other GPUs or cuBLAS versions; where it does not, the pad costs ~1.5 us
per call (6.7 against 4.9).

LoRA-enabled models retain the normal layer forward: the adapter wrapper is installed after
construction, and a raw base-weight projection would omit its contribution.

Env knobs this patch reads go through vllm.envs: VLLM_GDN_BA_PAD_SMALL_M (registered here).

